Rotavirus infection
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Abrupt recurrent vomiting, Profuse watery non-bloody diarrhoea, High fever, Rapid progression to severe dehydration

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-045
Reason for Visit: Abrupt onset of severe, unremitting vomiting and profuse watery diarrhoea in a young child, requiring urgent assessment for severe acute dehydration and hypovolaemic shock.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (child) and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a young, unvaccinated child experiencing forceful, recurrent vomiting preventing any fluid retention, accompanied by continuous watery diarrhoea and high fever. Urgent physical assessment is required to physically evaluate the degree of clinical dehydration, assess for impending hypovolaemic shock, and initiate immediate aggressive rehydration prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Parent states: "She has been violently vomiting every 20 minutes for hours, the diarrhoea is pure water, and she hasn't peed since yesterday."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced abruptly 12 hours ago with severe, recurrent, and forceful vomiting, which typically preceded the onset of the diarrhoea. Over the last 6 hours, the child developed profuse, watery, and distinctly non-bloody diarrhoea. The parents report a high fever, generalized severe fatigue, and profound systemic malaise. The child is experiencing frequent, severe abdominal cramping and distinct periumbilical pain. Crucially, the parents report decreased urinary output (no wet nappy in 12 hours), indicating rapid progression to severe clinical dehydration. The child is an unvaccinated 2-year-old (missed routine rotavirus schedule). Complete absence of macroscopic faecal blood or distinct mucus.
Functional Status: Highly lethargic, lying limply in parent's arms, irritable when moved.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe hypovolaemia in a small child; parents are highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 | HR: 160 | Temp: 39.2 | RR: 28 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 12 Kg
General: Lethargic, ill-appearing, pale child. Eyes appear sunken.
Physical Exam: Hydration assessment reveals classic signs of severe dehydration: deeply sunken eyes, completely dry mucous membranes (no saliva pool), absence of tears when crying, and extremely poor skin turgor (skin tenting on the abdomen). Capillary refill is severely delayed at 4 seconds. Abdomen is soft, with hyperactive bowel sounds and diffuse mild periumbilical tenderness, but absolute absence of focal severe abdominal peritonism or rigidity. Cardiovascular exam confirms severe tachycardia indicating profound hypovolaemia. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Abrupt recurrent vomiting, Profuse watery non-bloody diarrhoea, High fever, and Rapid progression to severe dehydration.
Environmental Observations: Multiple soiled towels and emesis basins in the room.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Missed rotavirus oral vaccine.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Acute Viral Gastroenteritis (Presumed Rotavirus) - Status: Exacerbated (Severe Dehydration/Impending Shock)
Differentials:

Intussusception - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound, continuous large-volume watery diarrhoea and continuous vomiting, rather than the classic intermittent, severe colicky pain with "red currant jelly" (bloody/mucus) stools, though careful abdominal palpation is required.

Bacterial Enteritis (Salmonella/Campylobacter) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absolute absence of macroscopic blood or severe inflammatory mucus in the stool, and the massive prominence of vomiting over just isolated diarrhoea.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The early, prominent, high-volume vomiting and profuse watery diarrhoea preceding vague periumbilical pain, without localized right lower quadrant peritonism.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Impending hypovolaemic shock)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The child presents with a fulminant acute gastroenteritis characterized by massive, rapid fluid loss through intractable vomiting and profuse watery diarrhoea. The physical examination confirms severe clinical dehydration (sunken eyes, dry mucous membranes, prolonged capillary refill, anuria) and severe tachycardia. In a young child, this degree of intravascular volume depletion can rapidly and catastrophically progress to hypovolaemic shock and acute renal failure. Immediate on-site clinical evaluation was mandatory to recognize the profound shock state and coordinate an immediate priority ambulance transfer to a pediatric emergency department for life-saving intravenous or intraosseous fluid resuscitation. This intervention strictly meets the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: IMMEDIATE call to 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport to a Pediatric Emergency Department. The child requires urgent, rapid intravenous (IV) fluid bolus resuscitation (e.g., 20mL/kg Normal Saline) to restore circulating volume. Attempt to administer a sublingual or buccal antiemetic (e.g., Ondansetron wafer) while awaiting transport to halt the severe vomiting reflex, but do not force large volumes of oral fluids as it will trigger further retching.
Positive Behavioral Change: Not applicable for the infant. Parents directed to keep the child laying flat to protect brain perfusion and gather a soiled nappy to take to the hospital for definitive viral antigen (EIA/PCR) stool testing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to receiving Emergency Department physician and paramedics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the child becomes completely unresponsive, unrousable, or begins to seize, the parents must immediately roll the child into the recovery position to prevent aspiration of vomit and notify the 000 dispatcher.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly confident severe viral gastroenteritis; child is hemodynamically unstable.
Clinical Course: Rotavirus causes massive, explosive fluid losses. The child requires immediate hospital admission for intravenous fluids until the vomiting stops and she can tolerate oral rehydration.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your child has lost a critical amount of body fluid and is dangerously dehydrated. Because she cannot keep anything down, she must go to the hospital immediately to receive fluids directly into her veins to support her blood pressure and kidneys.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A severe virus has inflamed her stomach and intestines, causing her to lose water massively from both ends. Young children have very little fluid reserve, which is why her eyes look sunken and her heart is racing. The paramedics will transport her safely, and the hospital will use an IV drip to rapidly replace the exact salts and water she has lost.
Actionable Steps: Keep her lying completely flat. Do not try to force her to drink large cups of water right now, as it will cause violent vomiting and worsen the dehydration. Wait for the paramedics.

